WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:06.155
Data often arrives in pieces, for example one file per enumerator or per month.

2
00:00:06.155 --> 00:00:11.510
In this lesson, you will learn to stack those pieces into one table with

3
00:00:11.510 --> 00:00:16.100
Append, and check that nothing is lost by counting rows. Let's combine!

4
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:20.384
Here is a query called All.

5
00:00:20.384 --> 00:00:26.344
It combines two smaller queries, Ahmad and Mahmood, each holding part of the same survey.

6
00:00:26.344 --> 00:00:29.920
Column profile shows the values in the selected column.

7
00:00:47.500 --> 00:00:53.857
To check the total, select the query, and on the Transform tab choose Count Rows. The answer:

8
00:00:53.857 --> 00:00:56.100
two thousand one hundred nine rows.

9
00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:01.506
Now let's build an append ourselves.

10
00:01:01.506 --> 00:01:06.186
The two source queries each had rows removed from the top and bottom, so

11
00:01:06.186 --> 00:01:08.860
each holds a different part of the data.

12
00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:22.526
Count Rows on one part gives a smaller number.

13
00:01:22.526 --> 00:01:26.560
Write down each count, so you can check the combined total later.

14
00:01:57.500 --> 00:02:01.155
On the Home tab, open Combine and choose Append Queries.

15
00:02:01.155 --> 00:02:05.540
Choose Two tables, then pick the table to append from the list.

16
00:02:15.500 --> 00:02:18.591
Click OK, and a new step appears: Appended Query.

17
00:02:18.591 --> 00:02:23.400
The rows of the second table now sit under the rows of the first.

18
00:02:47.500 --> 00:02:48.491
Count Rows again.

19
00:02:48.491 --> 00:02:51.796
The total should equal the sum of the two parts.

20
00:02:51.796 --> 00:02:54.440
If it does, nothing was lost. Great habit!

21
00:03:00.500 --> 00:03:00.886
Remember:

22
00:03:00.886 --> 00:03:05.898
append works best when both tables have the same column names and types.

23
00:03:05.898 --> 00:03:07.440
Clean first, then append.

